WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:09.777
As I wrote in my New York Herald article, there was only one good explanation for those mysterious events at sea: a marine animal of enormous power.

2
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:15.190
"The great depths of the sea are entirely unknown to us," I wrote in the newspaper.

3
00:00:15.191 --> 00:00:20.761
"Who knows what creatures live twelve or fifteen miles below the surface of the ocean?

4
00:00:20.762 --> 00:00:26.682
There is, however, one known animal powerful enough to pierce the hull of a steamship.

5
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:32.602
The common narwhal, or sea unicorn, often grows to a length of sixty feet.

6
00:00:32.603 --> 00:00:39.856
Increase its size by five or ten times and you have a gigantic beast capable of causing great damage."

7
00:00:39.857 --> 00:00:44.412
The ordinary narwhal is armed with an ivory tusk as hard as steel.

8
00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:48.841
Some of these tusks have killed whales and penetrated ships' hulls.

9
00:00:48.842 --> 00:00:56.658
One Paris museum possesses a narwhal tusk that is fifteen inches wide at its base and almost seven feet long.

10
00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:04.158
Now suppose that this tusk were six times stronger than usual and the animal ten times more powerful.

11
00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:10.158
Launch this weapon at high speed, and you would have a force capable of producing a catastrophe at sea.

12
00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:18.150
"Until I receive more information," I concluded in my newspaper article, "I believe that the beast is a narwhal of colossal dimensions.

13
00:01:18.151 --> 00:01:23.793
Unless, of course, it's something above and beyond anything that anyone has ever seen or imagined."

14
00:01:23.794 --> 00:01:27.539
I realized that I was undercutting my argument with this last sentence.

15
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:34.476
But I wanted to protect my reputation as a distinguished professor and not give the Americans any more reasons to laugh at me.

16
00:01:34.477 --> 00:01:38.809
After all, I had just admitted that I believed in a sea monster.

17
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:45.714
My article was very popular, and soon everyone seemed to have an opinion about the gigantic narwhal.

18
00:01:45.715 --> 00:01:54.714
I still thought that it could exist. If huge dinosaurs had once roamed the earth, why couldn't an equally large narwhal cruise the seas?

19
00:01:54.715 --> 00:01:59.127
Some people saw the creature's existence as a purely scientific problem.

20
00:01:59.128 --> 00:02:06.349
But more practical people, especially in England and the United States, were determined to rid the ocean of this beast.

21
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.587
The sea must be made safe for travelers.

22
00:02:09.588 --> 00:02:16.674
The United States led the way in this effort. In New York preparations were made for an expedition to pursue the narwhal.

23
00:02:16.675 --> 00:02:22.524
The warship Abraham Lincoln was armed and ready to depart under the command of Captain Farragut.

24
00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:30.262
But as soon as it was decided to go after the narwhal, the creature disappeared! For two months no ship encountered it.

25
00:02:30.263 --> 00:02:36.000
No one knew where to look for it. The Abraham Lincoln remained at the pier in Brooklyn.

26
00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:48.072
Suddenly on June 2, a steamship on its way from California to Shanghai reported that it had seen the animal three weeks earlier in the northern part of the Pacific Ocean.

27
00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:53.905
Everyone was extremely excited. Captain Farragut did not want to waste another minute.

28
00:02:53.906 --> 00:03:01.230
The Abraham Lincoln was quickly stocked with food and coal, and I soon received the following letter at my New York hotel:

29
00:03:01.231 --> 00:03:03.192
Dear Professor Aronnax:

30
00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:10.976
The government of the United States invites you to join the Abraham Lincoln expedition as a representative from France.

31
00:03:10.977 --> 00:03:19.222
Captain Farragut will provide a cabin for your use during the expedition. The ship will be departing in three hours.

32
00:03:19.223 --> 00:03:20.832
Very cordially yours,

33
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.051
J.B. Hobson

34
00:03:22.052 --> 00:03:24.873
Secretary of the U.S. Navy

35
00:03:24.874 --> 00:03:31.206
Three seconds before this letter arrived, I had no intention of pursuing the gigantic narwhal.

36
00:03:31.207 --> 00:03:37.944
Three seconds after it arrived, I felt my whole purpose in life was to chase down this creature. I

37
00:03:37.945 --> 00:03:41.265
was still tired from my long trip to Nebraska. I

38
00:03:41.266 --> 00:03:48.365
wanted nothing more than to see my country, my friends, and my little apartment near the botanical gardens. But

39
00:03:48.366 --> 00:03:50.311
nothing could hold me back. I

40
00:03:50.312 --> 00:03:57.682
forgot everything—fatigue, friends, and home—and accepted without hesitation the offer from the American government. "Conseil!"

41
00:03:57.683 --> 00:04:01.190
I called in an impatient voice. Conseil

42
00:04:01.191 --> 00:04:07.754
was my servant, who had accompanied me on all my travels. I liked him and he liked me too. For

43
00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:16.159
the last ten years, Conseil, whose name means advice in French, had followed me wherever science had led me. Not

44
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.246
once did he complain about the length of any journey. He

45
00:04:20.247 --> 00:04:27.238
was ready to pack his suitcase—and mine—for wherever we might be headed, whether to China or the Congo. Conseil

46
00:04:27.239 --> 00:04:35.333
was a quiet young man, never upset by any surprises that life threw our way, and adept at any task required of him. He

47
00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:40.738
had even become an expert in putting plants and animals into their scientific classes. At

48
00:04:40.739 --> 00:04:44.389
the age of thirty, he was ten years younger than I. "Conseil!"

49
00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:49.897
I called again as I began my feverish preparations for the journey. I

50
00:04:49.898 --> 00:04:56.897
usually didn't ask if it were convenient for him to accompany me, but this expedition could be hazardous. "Conseil!"

51
00:04:56.898 --> 00:04:59.970
I called a third time. "Did

52
00:04:59.971 --> 00:05:03.875
you call me?" he asked, entering the room. "Yes,"

53
00:05:03.876 --> 00:05:10.970
I replied. "Pack up all our things; we're leaving in two hours. There's not a moment to lose." "And

54
00:05:10.971 --> 00:05:15.050
what about your collections—the fossils and animal skins?" "The

55
00:05:15.051 --> 00:05:18.375
hotel will take care of them for us," I said. "And

56
00:05:18.376 --> 00:05:21.963
your live wild Indonesian swine?" "Someone

57
00:05:21.964 --> 00:05:28.780
at the hotel will feed it during our absence," I answered. "I will give orders to forward everything to France." "We're

58
00:05:28.781 --> 00:05:31.193
not returning to Paris?" "Eventually."

59
00:05:31.194 --> 00:05:38.256
I outlined our expedition on the Abraham Lincoln and asked if he wished to accompany me this time. "Whatever

60
00:05:38.257 --> 00:05:42.351
you do, Master, I'll do," Conseil replied. I

61
00:05:42.352 --> 00:05:48.439
urged him to think over his decision. "There's an element of danger," I said. "This

62
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.105
is one of those trips that people don't always survive." "Whatever

63
00:05:52.106 --> 00:05:55.169
you wish," Conseil said calmly. He

64
00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:59.010
soon had our bags packed, and we settled our hotel bill. I

65
00:05:59.011 --> 00:06:04.907
left instructions for shipping my containers of stuffed animals and dried plants to Paris. I

66
00:06:04.908 --> 00:06:12.073
arranged for the purchase of supplies for the care of my swine, and with Conseil at my heels, I jumped into a cab. At

67
00:06:12.074 --> 00:06:17.081
the dock in Brooklyn, our luggage was immediately taken to the deck of the Abraham Lincoln .

68
00:06:17.082 --> 00:06:24.898
We hurried onboard and I asked for Captain Farragut. One of the sailors led me to a small deck on the stern of the ship.

69
00:06:24.899 --> 00:06:31.414
A handsome officer held out his hand to me. "Professor Pierre Aronnax?" he said.

70
00:06:31.415 --> 00:06:34.970
"Yes, and you must be Captain Farragut."

71
00:06:34.971 --> 00:06:40.049
The officer nodded. "Welcome, Professor. Your cabin is ready for you."

72
00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:46.208
I let Conseil stow away our luggage while I watched Captain Farragut give the orders for the ship to cast off.

73
00:06:46.209 --> 00:06:52.795
A few minutes later and we would have missed the boat! The Abraham Lincoln moved majestically away from the pier.

74
00:06:52.796 --> 00:06:57.200
The docks on both sides of the East River were crowded with spectators.

75
00:06:57.201 --> 00:07:03.866
Half a million people cheered and waved their handkerchiefs as the warship moved down the river, toward the harbor.

76
00:07:03.867 --> 00:07:13.127
By nightfall we had left the lights of the city behind and were running at full steam into the dark waters of the Atlantic.

